  "metadata": {
    "title": "Is the Market Underestimating Meta Stock’s AI Payoff",
    "byline": null,
    "published_at": null,
    "site_name": "finance.yahoo.com",
    "language": null
  },
  "content": {
    "text": "Forget the far-off promises of super-intelligent agents for a moment; the company's massive AI investment is already quietly upgrading its core money-maker.\nIf you’re a Meta Platforms (META) investor, you’ve probably been staring at a stock that’s fallen over the last year and a capital spending forecast that looks like a typo. After a rough twelve months that saw the stock return -13.5%, management announced it was boosting its capital expenditures to a staggering range of $125 billion to $145 billion. A record-breaking number. But is it a breakthrough investment, or a balance-sheet breaking distraction?\nThe Grand Vision Is A Distraction\nListen to the company, and you’ll hear a grand narrative about building “personal super intelligence” and AI agents that will transform society. It’s an exciting vision. It’s also a distant one. Management readily admits they “don't think we have a very precise plan for exactly how each product is going to scale.” That kind of uncertainty, parked next to a historic spending plan, is what keeps investors up at night.\nBut focusing on that long-term ambition misses the more immediate, and arguably more powerful, story. The most compelling case for Meta’s stock has little to do with a far-future AI assistant. It’s about how that same AI research is already being plugged directly into the engine room: the core advertising business that generated $55 billion in revenue last quarter alone.\nAn Ad Machine On Upgraded Fuel\nThe evidence is piling up that these investments are delivering tangible, quantifiable results right now. In the first quarter, AI-driven enhancements to its ad models “drove a more than 6% increase in conversion rate for landing page view ads,” according to the company’s CFO. That’s not a forecast; it’s a result. Advertisers using Meta’s generative AI tools for video creative are seeing “more than 3% higher conversion rates in tests.”\nBeyond simply tweaking algorithms, this investment is creating entirely new, and rapidly growing, revenue streams. The company’s value optimization suite, which uses AI to help advertisers find the most valuable customers, now has an annual revenue run rate of over $20 billion, a figure that management notes is “more than doubling year-over-year.” Meanwhile, weekly conversations with business AIs on its messaging platforms have exploded, growing 10x since the start of this year. These are not small, experimental projects; they are signs of an AI-fueled upgrade taking hold across the business.\nWhat To Watch Now\nOf course, the spending is immense, and management has acknowledged that they have “continued to underestimate our compute needs.” The risk that costs could keep escalating without a clear payoff for the more futuristic projects is real. But the bull case doesn't require you to believe in a world run by AI agents just yet.",
